  "ground_truth": {
    "defect": true,
    "category": "contract-mismatch",
    "accepted_categories": [
      "logic-error"
    ],
    "severity": [
      "medium",
      "high"
    ],
    "explanation": "Cursor pagination is only correct when the cursor predicate and the ORDER BY walk the same field. The repo changes the sort to updated_at (a reasonable dashboard feature), but the WHERE clause still filters created_at < cursor, and the handler still encodes last.created_at as nextCursor. Page 1 is now the 50 most recently *updated* tickets; its last element's created_at is an essentially arbitrary date, so page 2 (created_at < that date) skips every older-updated ticket whose created_at is newer than the boundary and can re-list tickets already shown. Users see silently missing and duplicated tickets from page 2 onward. The handler diff is cursor hardening (length cap, NaN guard) that reads as the pagination being actively maintained, and the new tests never fetch a second page, so the suite cannot catch it. The bug is only establishable by combining the repo's sort change, its unchanged WHERE field, and the handler's cursor encoding."
  },
